= A debate season, not one clean confrontation

Końskie begins as a proposed duel and fills with candidates who refuse to wait
outside. An all-candidate studio then gives each campaign reciprocal questions
and a timed statement. The joint broadcasters close the sequence with equal
clocks, thematic blocks and nearly four hours in which one weak answer can
erase a week of campaigning.

[? if pres_2025_united_left: **[+ pres_2025_left_candidate +]** stands at the
only Left lectern. Razem organisers are part of the same campaign; there is no
second Zandberg candidacy.?][? if not pres_2025_united_left: **[+
pres_2025_left_candidate +]** and <span class="party-razem"><b>Adrian Zandberg</b></span> occupy separate lecterns.
Their overlap is now a political argument the audience can compare.?]

Pre-debate support for **[+ pres_2025_left_candidate +]**: **[+
pres_2025_poll_left +]%**.

- @pres25_debate_security: Take the first lectern

@pres25_debate_security
title: Debate I — security and foreign policy
new-page: true
on-arrival: public_mood_pending_national_security_salience += 14; public_mood_pending_border_security_salience += 9

**[+ pres_2025_right_surname +]** promises a stronger national command; **[+
pres_2025_ko_surname +]** presents alliances and administrative competence;
**[+ pres_2025_konf_surname +]** says the existing parties spend before they
define a national interest. The
President commands the armed forces but does not write the budget alone. Our
first answer must show both resolve and restraint.

- @pres25_security_signature
- @pres25_security_law
- @pres25_security_hawk

@pres25_security_signature
title: [+ pres_2025_security_signature_title +]
subtitle: Use the nominee's own register; connect defence to the capacity of the civilian state.
on-arrival: {!
const scores = {
    "Magdalena Biejat": 2.7,
    "Agnieszka Dziemianowicz-Bąk": 3.0,
    "Adrian Zandberg": 3.1
};
Q.pres_2025_debate_score += scores[Q.pres_2025_left_candidate] || 2.7;
Q.pres_2025_debate_security_line = Q.pres_2025_security_signature_title;
Q.public_sector_support += 2;
Q.military_trust += 1;
Q.pres_2025_debate_round = 2;
Q.news_headline = Q.pres_2025_left_candidate +
    " joins national defence to a capable civilian state";
!}

The answer refuses the choice the moderator offered. Deterrence is described as
a whole system — shelters, hospitals with power, a border service that is
staffed, conscripts who are trained rather than photographed — and the
brigades are placed inside it rather than opposite it.

**[+ pres_2025_right_surname +]** replies that this is a civilian's idea of
defence. It lands with
the audience that has been asked to pay for the last three procurement
announcements without ever being told what happens to their own town.

- @pres25_debate_economy: The moderators turn to taxes and housing

@pres25_security_law
title: [+ pres_2025_security_law_title +]
subtitle: A constitutionally precise answer with a lower emotional ceiling.
on-arrival: pres_2025_debate_score += 2.4; pres_2025_debate_security_line = pres_2025_security_law_title; constitutional_restraint += 3; ko_relation += 1; pres_2025_debate_round = 2; public_mood_pending_rule_of_law_support += 7; public_mood_pending_national_security_support += 4; news_headline = "The Left defines the limits as well as the powers of the presidency"

The answer is exact: the President commands, but does not deploy alone; signs
ratifications, but does not invent obligations; speaks for Poland abroad within
what the Sejm has voted. It is the answer a lawyer would give, and the
constitutionalists in every camp write it down.

It is also the answer nobody repeats in a bar. The studio moves on without a
clip, and KO's spokesman is quietly grateful that the Left has just made his
own candidate's position respectable.

- @pres25_debate_economy: The moderators turn to taxes and housing

@pres25_security_hawk
title: [+ pres_2025_security_hawk_title +]
subtitle: Chase the right's frame; gains a hard headline and surrenders fiscal and social ground.
on-arrival: pres_2025_debate_score += 1.1; pres_2025_debate_security_line = pres_2025_security_hawk_title; military_trust += 3; fiscal_stress += 2; labor_credibility -= 2; far_right_agenda += 2; pres_2025_debate_round = 2; public_mood_pending_national_security_support += 9; public_mood_pending_refugee_solidarity_support += -6; news_headline = "The Left tries to outbid the right on its own security frame"

The bid is made on the right's own ground and on the right's own terms: more
than they promised, sooner than they promised. The generals in the studio
audience approve. So does the applause line.

The trouble arrives four minutes later, when the economy block opens and the
same nominee has to explain which budget the extra brigades came out of.
**[+ pres_2025_right_surname +]** has been outbid on a question the PiS-backed
candidate was never going to lose, and the Left
has spent its fiscal argument to do it.

- @pres25_debate_economy: The moderators turn to taxes and housing

@pres25_debate_economy
title: Debate II — taxes, housing and public services
new-page: true
on-arrival: public_mood_pending_social_spending_salience += 12

**[+ pres_2025_konf_surname +]** says the established parties tax without a
purpose and spend without a national plan.
**[+ pres_2025_right_surname +]** promises protection without a bill; **[+
pres_2025_ko_surname +]** lists investments and asks voters to trust the
governing camp to finish them. The Left has one minute to make its economic
conflict intelligible outside a party programme.

- @pres25_economy_signature
- @pres25_economy_mentzen
- @pres25_economy_centre

@pres25_economy_signature
title: [+ pres_2025_economy_signature_title +]
subtitle: Define prosperity in the nominee's strongest voice and keep ownership of the answer.
on-arrival: {!
const scores = {
    "Magdalena Biejat": 3.1,
    "Agnieszka Dziemianowicz-Bąk": 3.2,
    "Adrian Zandberg": 3.3
};
Q.pres_2025_debate_score += scores[Q.pres_2025_left_candidate] || 3;
Q.pres_2025_debate_economy_line = Q.pres_2025_economy_signature_title;
Q.labor_credibility += 3;
Q.household_policy_pressure += 2;
Q.pres_2025_debate_round = 3;
Q.news_headline = Q.pres_2025_left_candidate +
    " forces the economic debate onto the cost of ordinary life";
!}

The minute is spent on three numbers: what a room costs in a city where a
nurse works, what an hour of care costs a family that has no one at home, and
what is left of a salary once both are paid. No tax rate is mentioned until the
last ten seconds, when it arrives as an answer rather than a subject.

[+ pres_2025_konf_surname +] is left arguing against the price of housing as though it were a
government policy he opposes, which for a moment it sounds like. The
after-broadcast panels lead on the figures rather than on the exchange, and the
figures are ours.

- @pres25_debate_rights: The third block joins rights to presidential power

@pres25_economy_mentzen
title: [+ pres_2025_economy_mentzen_title +]
subtitle: A direct counterattack can travel further than the complete answer.
on-arrival: {!
