Q.news_headline = Q.pres_2025_left_candidate +
    " makes " + Q.pres_2025_konf_surname +
    " price the state behind the programme";
!}

The question is put twice, because the first answer is a slogan about waste
and national purpose. Which hospital. Which school. Which pension. **[+
pres_2025_konf_surname +]** explains the programme and still declines to name
the institution expected to carry it.

The exchange travels. It also makes the night about **[+
pres_2025_konf_surname +]**, and there is a
version of the evening in which our own programme is now the thing nobody was
asked to describe.

- @pres25_debate_rights: The third block joins rights to presidential power

@pres25_economy_centre
title: [+ pres_2025_economy_centre_title +]
subtitle: Easier coalition language; less reason to choose the Left over its neighbours.
on-arrival: pres_2025_debate_score += 1.5; pres_2025_debate_economy_line = pres_2025_economy_centre_title; ko_relation += 2; p2050_relation += 2; party_unity -= 1; labor_credibility -= 1; pres_2025_debate_round = 3; news_headline = "The nominee offers the centre a social promise it can absorb"

The promise is sized so that a KO minister could make it without a fight in
cabinet, and by Tuesday one of them has. The centre finds the Left reasonable;
the panels describe a candidate who has learned to govern.

Our own organisers watch a policy leave the building. If the same commitment
can be had from the larger party, the argument for a separate ballot line has
just been made a little harder to state.

- @pres25_debate_rights: The third block joins rights to presidential power

