@pres25_rights_signature
title: [+ pres_2025_rights_signature_title +]
subtitle: State the candidate's full commitment and accept the conservative counter-mobilisation.
on-arrival: {!
const scores = {
    "Magdalena Biejat": 3.3,
    "Agnieszka Dziemianowicz-Bąk": 3.0,
    "Adrian Zandberg": 3.0
};
Q.pres_2025_debate_score += scores[Q.pres_2025_left_candidate] || 3;
Q.pres_2025_debate_rights_line = Q.pres_2025_rights_signature_title;
Q.progressive_credibility += 3;
Q.feminist_trust += 3;
Q.barons_dissent += 2;
Q.pres_2025_debate_round = 4;
Q.news_headline = Q.pres_2025_left_candidate +
    " makes equal citizenship a test of the presidential signature";
!}

The commitment is made in the first sentence rather than the last: this pen
signs a partnership act, this pen signs a legal-recognition procedure, this pen
does not sign a border regime that suspends the right to ask for asylum. No
conditional clauses, no timetable that outlives a presidency.

The counter-mobilisation begins the same night — parish bulletins, a **[+
pres_2025_right_surname +]** line about institutional radicalism, three days
of comment sections. So does
the other kind of response: organisations that have been asked to wait since
2023 now have a sentence they can hold somebody to.

- @pres25_debate_questions: The all-candidate format opens the questions

@pres25_rights_record
title: [+ pres_2025_rights_record_title +]
subtitle: Answer the question and prosecute the gap between promises and power.
on-arrival: {!
const scores = {
    "Magdalena Biejat": 2.8,
    "Agnieszka Dziemianowicz-Bąk": 2.6,
    "Adrian Zandberg": 3.4
};
Q.pres_2025_debate_score += scores[Q.pres_2025_left_candidate] || 2.8;
Q.pres_2025_debate_rights_line = Q.pres_2025_rights_record_title;
Q.pres_2025_debate_target = "ko";
Q.progressive_credibility += 2;
Q.ko_relation -= 3;
Q.psl_relation -= 2;
Q.pres_2025_debate_round = 4;
Q.news_headline = Q.pres_2025_left_candidate +
    " makes the democratic camp answer for unfinished rights promises";
!}

The record is read out as a list with dates attached: what was promised in
October 2023, what reached committee, what was withdrawn the week before a vote
and on whose insistence. [? if left_in_government: It is an awkward list to read
from inside the coalition, and the nominee reads it anyway, which is the part
that registers.?][? if not left_in_government: It is the list an opposition
exists to read, and nobody on the platform can claim it is invented.?]

**[+ pres_2025_ko_surname +]** answers that these are parliamentary matters.
That is precisely
the sentence the list was constructed to draw out of him, and it will be
replayed more often than anything either of them said about rights.

- @pres25_debate_questions: The all-candidate format opens the questions

@pres25_rights_procedure
title: [+ pres_2025_rights_procedure_title +]
subtitle: Constitutionally safe and politically narrow; movements hear another deferral.
on-arrival: pres_2025_debate_score += 1.2; pres_2025_debate_rights_line = pres_2025_rights_procedure_title; constitutional_restraint += 2; feminist_trust -= 3; progressives_dissent += 3; pres_2025_debate_round = 4; news_headline = "The Left narrows the rights answer to presidential procedure"

The answer is about process: a president receives a bill, a president has
twenty-one days, a president may refer a statute to a court that must be
repaired first. Every clause of it is true and none of it says what the pen
would do.

**[+ pres_2025_right_surname +]** does not bother to attack it. The women's
organisations that spent the
spring supplying the campaign with volunteers hear the same deferral they have
heard since the autumn of 2023, and some of them stop returning calls before
the week is out.

- @pres25_debate_questions: The all-candidate format opens the questions

@pres25_debate_questions
title: Debate IV — choose whom to question
new-page: true

In the all-candidate format each campaign chooses opponents and the organisers
draw another pairing. A question can expose a rival, but it also names the
contest our campaign wants viewers to remember.

[? if not pres_2025_united_left: Zandberg is a legitimate rival for the same
Left electorate. Questioning him turns the organisational split into the
night's central argument.?][? if pres_2025_united_left: Zandberg is part of our
common campaign and cannot be selected as an opponent. The question must cross
an actual campaign boundary.?]

- @pres25_question_nawrocki
- @pres25_question_mentzen
- @pres25_question_trzaskowski
- @pres25_question_zandberg

@pres25_question_nawrocki
title: Ask [+ pres_2025_right_surname +] which PiS appointment would ever be refused
subtitle: Makes the independence of the PiS-backed candidacy the test.
on-arrival: {!
Q.pres_2025_debate_score += 2.5;
Q.pres_2025_debate_question_line = Q.pres_2025_right_surname +
    " must define independence from the party that built the campaign";
Q.pres_2025_debate_target = "right";
Q.judicial_repair_mandate += 2;
Q.pis_relation -= 2;
Q.media_capacity += 1;
Q.pres_2025_debate_round = 5;
Q.news_headline = Q.pres_2025_right_surname +
    " is asked to name the limit of the debt to PiS";
!}

One appointment. Any appointment. A name the candidate would send back to
Nowogrodzka with a refusal. **[+ pres_2025_right_surname +]** promises to
appoint the best people, is asked again, and does not accept the premise.

The civic committee spends the next two days explaining that a candidate is
not obliged to invent a quarrel with his own supporters. That explanation is
the answer, and everyone watching now has it.

- @pres25_debate_close: The closing statements

@pres25_question_mentzen
title: Ask [+ pres_2025_konf_surname +] what happens to the hospital, school and pension
subtitle: Forces a material answer and competes directly for anti-establishment voters.
on-arrival: {!
Q.pres_2025_debate_score += 3;
Q.pres_2025_debate_question_line = Q.pres_2025_konf_surname +
    " must put a service behind the programme";
Q.pres_2025_debate_target = "mentzen";
Q.labor_credibility += 2;
Q.konf_relation -= 2;
Q.pres_2025_debate_round = 5;
Q.news_headline = Q.pres_2025_konf_surname +
    " is made to put a functioning service behind the programme";
!}

The question comes with a figure already attached, which is what makes it
unanswerable in ninety seconds: the pledge costs this much, the health fund
spends that much, name the difference. **[+ pres_2025_konf_surname +]** reaches for efficiency, then
for bureaucracy, then for the clock.

His own voters are not scandalised — they came for the tax line, not for the
arithmetic. But the young men who came for the anti-establishment tone have now
watched him behave exactly like every minister he complains about, and some of
them are precisely the electorate we are trying to reach.

- @pres25_debate_close: The closing statements

@pres25_question_trzaskowski
title: Ask [+ pres_2025_ko_surname +] which social promise the camp will deliver first
subtitle: Challenges KO without treating [+ pres_2025_right_surname +] as an acceptable alternative.
on-arrival: {!
Q.pres_2025_debate_score += 2.6;
Q.pres_2025_debate_question_line = Q.pres_2025_ko_surname +
    " must name the first promise that survives coalition government";
Q.pres_2025_debate_target = "ko";
Q.ko_relation -= 3;
Q.public_trust += 1;
Q.pres_2025_debate_round = 5;
Q.news_headline = Q.pres_2025_ko_surname +
    " is asked to convert democratic urgency into a delivery date";
!}

The question is framed so that it cannot be heard as help for the other camp:
not whether the democratic coalition deserves to win, but which of its promises
has a date. **[+ pres_2025_ko_surname +]** names three, without dates, and adds
that governing is a matter of sequencing.

KO's campaign staff are furious in the corridor afterwards, and their fury is
itself informative: the one thing the democratic camp cannot afford in this
election is to be asked to be specific in public.

- @pres25_debate_close: The closing statements

@pres25_question_zandberg
title: Ask Zandberg what opposition can deliver before the next election
subtitle: A real Left leadership contest; unavailable when Zandberg is the common nominee.
view-if: pres_2025_united_left = 0 and pres_2025_left_candidate != "Adrian Zandberg"
on-arrival: pres_2025_debate_score += 2.2; pres_2025_debate_question_line = "Zandberg must price the freedom of opposition against the power to implement"; pres_2025_debate_target = "razem"; razem_cooperation -= 4; party_unity -= 2; media_capacity += 2; pres_2025_debate_round = 5; news_headline = "The two Left campaigns turn governing power into a direct argument"

The exchange is the sharpest of the night because both people in it are
serious. Zandberg answers that a programme kept intact is worth more than a
ministry spent defending compromises; the reply is that a programme nobody has
ever had to sign is not yet a programme.

Neither of them loses. What loses is the shared field operation: by the morning
both sets of organisers have watched their candidates treat the other campaign
as the opponent, and that is not a thing a press release takes back.

- @pres25_debate_close: The closing statements

@pres25_debate_close
title: Debate V — ninety seconds without a prompt
new-page: true

**[+ pres_2025_right_surname +]** closes on national continuity. **[+
pres_2025_ko_surname +]** asks the democratic camp to finish the change of
government. **[+ pres_2025_centre_surname +]** offers distance from both.
Stanowski reminds viewers that the format itself is part of his case. The final
ninety seconds can define our candidacy or borrow the conflict of a larger
rival.

- @pres25_close_signature
- @pres25_close_right
- @pres25_close_duopoly

@pres25_close_signature
title: [+ pres_2025_close_signature_title +]
subtitle: Keep the last word on the nominee's own political ground.
on-arrival: {!
const scores = {
    "Magdalena Biejat": 3.2,
    "Agnieszka Dziemianowicz-Bąk": 3.3,
    "Adrian Zandberg": 3.5
};
Q.pres_2025_debate_score += scores[Q.pres_2025_left_candidate] || 3.1;
Q.pres_2025_debate_close_line = Q.pres_2025_close_signature_title;
Q.pres_2025_debate_strategy = "Own ground";
Q.labor_credibility += 2;
Q.organiser_energy += 2;
Q.news_headline = Q.pres_2025_left_candidate +
    " closes the debate on the Left's own ground";
!}

Ninety seconds and no opponent named. The statement describes a country the
campaign is asking for rather than the two men it is standing between, and it
ends early, which almost nobody does.

Whether it works is a question for the panels. What it certainly does is give
every organiser in the country a paragraph they can repeat at a door without
having to explain the PiS-backed candidate first.

- @pres25_debate_verdict: The spin room

@pres25_close_right
title: [+ pres_2025_close_right_title +]
subtitle: Strong anti-PiS transfer appeal; less independent ownership of the final minute.
on-arrival: pres_2025_debate_score += 2.5; pres_2025_debate_close_line = pres_2025_close_right_title; pres_2025_debate_strategy = "Indictment of the right"; pres_2025_debate_target = "right"; ko_relation += 2; pis_relation -= 2; news_headline = "The final minute becomes an indictment of the PiS-backed presidency"

The last minute is spent on what a **[+ pres_2025_right_surname +]** presidency
would sign and what it
would refuse to sign, item by item, in the present tense. It is the clearest
warning anyone gives all evening, and half the democratic camp shares it before
midnight.

They share it, however, as a warning about **[+ pres_2025_right_surname +]**.
By the second day of
coverage the clip is being used to argue that the serious anti-PiS ballot is
the largest one, which was not the intention.

- @pres25_debate_verdict: The spin room

@pres25_close_duopoly
title: [+ pres_2025_close_duopoly_title +]
subtitle: Contest both leading camps; a sharper independent identity with fewer easy transfers.
on-arrival: pres_2025_debate_score += 2.8; pres_2025_debate_close_line = pres_2025_close_duopoly_title; pres_2025_debate_strategy = "Against both blocs"; ko_relation -= 2; pis_relation -= 2; party_unity += 1; news_headline = "The Left refuses to donate its final minute to the leading pair"

Both lecterns are named and both are charged with the same thing: an agreement,
never signed and never broken, that the argument in Poland will be about
symbols because the argument about money has been settled in advance.

It is the most quotable ninety seconds of the night and the least useful to
anybody else, which is the point. It also guarantees that neither large camp
will be doing us any favours in a fortnight.

- @pres25_debate_verdict: The spin room

@pres25_debate_verdict
title: The spin room
subtitle: Commit the debate result before seeing the final tracking poll.
new-page: true
on-arrival: {!
const candidateExperience25 =
    Q.pres_2025_left_candidate == "Adrian Zandberg" ? 1.0 :
    (Q.pres_2025_left_candidate == "Agnieszka Dziemianowicz-Bąk" ? 0.4 : 0.2);
const debateVariance25 =
    ((Number(Q.pres_2025_debate_roll) || 0.5) - 0.5) *
    (Q.hard_mode ? 2.8 : 2.3);
Q.pres_2025_debate_effective_score =
    (Number(Q.pres_2025_debate_score) || 0) +
    candidateExperience25 + debateVariance25;
if (Q.pres_2025_debate_effective_score >= 13) {
    Q.pres_2025_debate_outcome = "Breakthrough performance";
    Q.pres_2025_debate_bonus =
        Q.pres_2025_left_candidate == "Adrian Zandberg" ? 1.6 : 1.25;
    Q.public_trust += 4;
    Q.media_capacity += 4;
} else if (Q.pres_2025_debate_effective_score >= 10) {
    Q.pres_2025_debate_outcome = "Clear debate win";
    Q.pres_2025_debate_bonus =
        Q.pres_2025_left_candidate == "Adrian Zandberg" ? 1.05 : 0.8;
    Q.public_trust += 2;
    Q.media_capacity += 2;
} else if (Q.pres_2025_debate_effective_score >= 7) {
    Q.pres_2025_debate_outcome = "Mixed but credible";
    Q.pres_2025_debate_bonus = 0.3;
} else {
    Q.pres_2025_debate_outcome = "Lost in the field";
    Q.pres_2025_debate_bonus = -0.35;
    Q.public_trust -= 2;
    Q.media_capacity -= 1;
}

// A month of debates does not only move our own number. A strong night takes
// something off the campaign we spent it attacking; a weak one hands the
// week's dominant clip to whichever rival was standing closest.
const rivalShift25 = function(key, amount) {
    if (!key) return;
    Q["pres_2025_rival_shift_" + key] =
        (Number(Q["pres_2025_rival_shift_" + key]) || 0) + amount;
};
const strongNight25 =
    Q.pres_2025_debate_outcome == "Breakthrough performance" ||
    Q.pres_2025_debate_outcome == "Clear debate win";
if (strongNight25 && Q.pres_2025_debate_target) {
    rivalShift25(
        Q.pres_2025_debate_target,
        Q.pres_2025_debate_outcome == "Breakthrough performance"
            ? -0.45
            : -0.28
    );
}
if (strongNight25 && Q.pres_2025_debate_strategy == "Against both blocs") {
    rivalShift25("ko", -0.12);
    rivalShift25("right", -0.12);
}
const upsetRoll25 = Number.isFinite(Number(Q.pres_2025_debate_upset_roll))
    ? Number(Q.pres_2025_debate_upset_roll)
    : this.random.uint32() / 4294967296;
Q.pres_2025_debate_upset_roll = upsetRoll25;
const adverseNight25 =
    Q.pres_2025_debate_outcome == "Lost in the field" ||
    (
        Q.pres_2025_debate_outcome == "Mixed but credible" &&
        upsetRoll25 < 0.20
    );
if (adverseNight25) {
    const rivalGain25 =
        Q.pres_2025_debate_outcome == "Lost in the field" ? 0.55 : 0.40;
    const rivalIndex25 = Math.min(2, Math.floor(upsetRoll25 * 3));
    if (rivalIndex25 == 0) {
        rivalShift25("right", rivalGain25);
        Q.pres_2025_debate_upset =
            Q.pres_2025_right_surname +
            "'s calm becomes the clip the bulletins keep returning to";
    } else if (rivalIndex25 == 1) {
        rivalShift25("mentzen", rivalGain25);
        Q.pres_2025_debate_upset =
            Q.pres_2025_konf_surname +
            "'s counterattack travels furthest online";
    } else {
        rivalShift25("stanowski", rivalGain25);
        Q.pres_2025_debate_upset =
            "Stanowski turns the format itself into the night's most-shared moment";
    }
} else if (Q.pres_2025_debate_outcome == "Breakthrough performance") {
    Q.pres_2025_debate_upset =
        Q.pres_2025_left_candidate +
        " breaks out of a field built to make small campaigns inaudible";
} else {
    Q.pres_2025_debate_upset =
        "No rival clip displaces the Left's own lines from the coverage";
}

Q.news_headline = Q.pres_2025_left_candidate + ": " +
    Q.pres_2025_debate_outcome.toLowerCase() +
    " across the presidential debates";
!}

= [+ pres_2025_left_candidate +]: [+ pres_2025_debate_outcome +]

[? if pres_2025_debate_outcome = "Breakthrough performance": By the time the
last studio empties the season has produced something no amount of paid
advertising buys: a candidate the bulletins have to include in order to
describe the election. Editors who spent January treating the Left as a
courtesy row now send crews. Volunteers arrive at offices that did not ask for
them.?]
[? if pres_2025_debate_outcome = "Clear debate win": The panels agree, in the
careful way panels agree, that **[+ pres_2025_left_candidate +]** had the
better month. It does not rearrange the race, but it ends the assumption that
this candidacy is a formality, and the invitations for the last fortnight
arrive on different terms.?]
[? if pres_2025_debate_outcome = "Mixed but credible": Nothing broke. Nothing
carried either. The campaign leaves the season with a set of usable lines, a
nominee who was never embarrassed and no single moment large enough to make an
undecided voter change what they were going to do anyway.?]
[? if pres_2025_debate_outcome = "Lost in the field": Twelve lecterns and four
hours of airtime, and the honest summary is that our minutes were the ones
people used to make tea. Two answers are being replayed as evidence of
inexperience. Headquarters spends the following morning on damage control
rather than on the ground operation.?]

[+ pres_2025_debate_upset +].

**The argument the audience heard**

**Security:** [+ pres_2025_debate_security_line +]

**Economy:** [+ pres_2025_debate_economy_line +]

**Rights and presidential power:** [+ pres_2025_debate_rights_line +]

**Question to a rival:** [+ pres_2025_debate_question_line +]

**Closing statement:** [+ pres_2025_debate_close_line +]

[? if pres_2025_left_candidate = "Adrian Zandberg": Zandberg's previous
national-debate experience raises the floor and ceiling of the performance. It
does not create a second Razem opponent: this is the common campaign's result.?]

- @pres25_tracking_poll: Release the final tracking poll

